      "text": "To determine the probability that four fair 6-sided dice will show different numbers, we need to calculate the total number of possible outcomes and the number of favorable outcomes.\n\nFirst, we calculate the total number of outcomes. Since each die has 6 faces, the total number of outcomes for rolling four dice is:\n\n\\[\n6^4 = 1296\n\\]\n\nThis is because each of the four dice can land on any of the 6 faces independently.\n\nNext, we calculate the number of ways four dice can all show the same number. There are 6 possible numbers (1 through 6), and for each number, there is exactly 1 way for all four dice to show that number:\n\n\\[\n6\n\\]\n\nSo, there are 6 ways for all four dice to show the same number.\n\nTo find the number of outcomes in which the four dice show different numbers, we subtract the number of outcomes where all dice show the same number from the total number of outcomes:\n\n\\[\n1296 - 6 = 1290\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that the four dice show different numbers is the number of favorable outcomes over the total number of outcomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1290}{1296}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this fraction by dividing both the numerator and denominator by their greatest common divisor, which is 6:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1290 \\div 6}{1296 \\div 6} = \\frac{215}{216}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that the four dice will not all show the same number is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{215}{216}}\n\\]",
