      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the probability that when rolling four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number, we will use the complementary probability method. \n\nFirst, let's calculate the total number of possible outcomes when rolling four dice. Since each die has 6 faces, the total number of outcomes is 6^4 = 1296.\n\nNext, we need to find the number of outcomes in which all four dice show the same number. Since there are 6 possibilities for a number on a die (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, or 6), there are 6 outcomes where all four dice show the same number.\n\nNow, we can calculate the probability of all four dice showing the same number by dividing the number of outcomes by the total number of possible outcomes:\n\nP(all same number) = 6/1296 = 1/216\n\nWe can now use complementary probability to find the probability that all four dice won't show the same number. This is equal to 1 minus the probability that all four dice will show the same number:\n\nP(not all same number) = 1 - 1/216 = 215/216\n\nTherefore, the probability that when rolling four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number is 215/216.\n\n\\boxed{\\frac{215}{216}}",
